  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 314 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nHigh temperatures on Thursday will continue to remain above\nnormal. However, with the low-level thermal ridge weakening\nslightly, highs will ease back slightly into the mid 80s to mid\n90s. \n\nEnhanced moisture and lift continue to be focused over our western\nareas on Thursday into Friday and the WPC does show a level 2 of 4\nrisk for heavy rain across most of Val Verde County, with a level\n1 of 4 risk into the Rio Grande plains and southern Edwards\nPlateau. We will need to monitor the Thursday evening into early\nFriday morning period for the above mentioned areas as a strong\ncold front encounters the moist axis over the Big Bend/northern\nMexico. \n\nThe cold front continues to move southward rather quickly late\nThursday into early Friday morning. While the current NBM only\nextends rain chances eastward into the Hill Country, could see a\nscenario where a thin line of convection develops along the low-\nlevel forcing along/just behind the front. We will need to monitor\nand may need to add some low rain chances farther east into the \nI-35 corridor and coastal plains as the front moves through Friday\nmorning. \n\nIt will be quite windy behind the front on Friday and a few spots\nover the higher terrain of the Hill Country may briefly approach\nthe low end wind advisory threshold of sustained winds at or above\n26 mph. Much cooler temperatures and dry weather are in store for\nthe upcoming weekend. Weekend highs will be in the upper 60s to\nlower 80s for most of the region along with lows in the lower 50s\nto lower 60s. Some low rain chances may enter the forecast late\nMonday into Tuesday as southerly flow in the lower levels begins\nto reestablish, with southwest flow aloft. Any rain chances will\nremain low at this time.",
